you're the taken king is more than just  an expansion it's also a heart  transplant and when all is said and done  destiny is pulled through the operation  with renewed vigor and only a few scars  to show for it  Destiny's first year was a collage of  peaks and valleys as Bungie released two  expansions to a sci-fi multiplayer title  at its best  destiny was a phenomenal shooter with  pristine controls and thrilling  cooperative play but at its worst  destiny felt empty it's gorgeous world  held promise but lacked any soul  I returned to those static environments  for the chance of better gear however  slim it might be I continued long past  the point of actually having fun but the  taken King rectifies many of destiny's  problems the new light blue and  experienced systems have been revamped  to be more accessible and provide a more  rewarding overall experience light is no  longer tied to specific armor but now  it's just an average of your gears  overall value raising it is necessary  for difficult missions but when it comes  to your rank you can reach the level 40  cap through experience alone and in the  fact that loot scales with your level  and the promise of better gear is almost  always on the horizon with these new  systems in place  destiny allows you to play however you  want regardless of which missions you  want to play or how often you want to  play them you can play cooperative  strikes explore Mars or fight through  the expansions excellent new story  missions these quests follow our  Guardians and their fight against Oryx  the hive Cain whose presence has cast a  shadow over things for some time now and  for the first time these story missions  are coherent providing much-needed  context to the quests our Guardians  embark upon I always knew the mission  objective and always felt the pull of  that final battle with a taken king at  the end of the road and the story is  spurred on with vivid characterization  the vanguard leaders are more than just  talking mannequins now with  personalities motivations and fears of  their own nolan north delivers a stellar  performance as your ghost but it's  cayde-6  the hunter captain who stands out  ah taken eros get your rock off my map  voiced by Nathan Fillion his sarcasm and  sardonic wit are just facades they  slowly give way to doubt and remorse as  he learned to the death old friend and  for the first time  destiny shows signs of humanity in its  storytelling ironic considering cayde-6  isn't human  fighting through these story missions is  more varied than ever before it's not  just about endless shooting at cookie  cutter enemies the taking our ghosts  like clones of destinies other factions  which Oryx has absorbed into his own  army creating a force with a wide array  of abilities and attacks fallen  captain's blind you cabal mercenaries  force blast with her shield and vex  goblins shield their allies forcing you  to aim for one specific run at a time  Destiny's combat is now more dynamic and  more versatile than the skirmishes of  its first year the strike bosses in the  taken King follow suit and require  teamwork and tactics to defeat  the taking Kings PvP modes and maps are  excellent as well rift places a spark at  the center of the map and much like a  neutral capture-the-flag match both  teams must bring it in to the enemy base  mayhem on the other hand the most  creative new variant grants increased  super energy resulting in chaotic  display of flaming hammers electric  storms and void arrows sailing through  the air these modes play out across  eight new Maps each with their own  varied sight lines and verticality  Bungie once again has proven its prowess  with multiplayer level design and then  there's the new raid it's called king's  fall and it's the most expansive in  destiny to date it begins on a sour note  with ill-advised first-person  platforming but soon gathers speed  thereafter the checkpoints all required  careful teamwork and the bosses are some  of the most creative Bungie has ever  designed I don't want to spoil too much  about the six-person Trek but its final  boss both in size and scope is colossal  he's a fitting end to Destiny's most  dynamic raid yet but despite all the  improvements the destiny system's all  the new content and places to take in  King and all the heart that's gone into  this compelling storyline remnants of  Destiny's past still remain after  sixteen hours of the expansion the grind  began at first I didn't mind the taken  King excels in granting you gear through  hard work rather than luck but I played  the same strikes five times over day in  and day out i trudged through the  Dreadnought for hours on end i completed  similar bounties throughout the week and  didn't see many variations throughout 35  hours of the taken king for a game that  requires so much grinding it still  doesn't have the foundation to support  the repetition required the promise of  better loot is the driving force but the  journey there is taxing quests are  interesting only for a time but after a  while they once again become long list  of cloying chores but the taken King  still deserves ample praise it not only  grants us new content but reinvigorates  a game that otherwise lost its way in  one of the expansions early story  missions you'll come across the remains  of Destiny's very first strike boss he's  right where you left him surrounded by  his own robotic innards the life long  gone from his dormant shell and like the  Guardians who killed that boss destiny  has since moved on there are still  traces of its mediocre first year but  destiny is now more vital more promising  with much of its rotting corpse left  behind  remember when suffix Prime was our  biggest problem
 

